NAT - Network Address Translation
Converts an internal network address to a singular exterior (public) IP address
Called a "stateful firewall"
Means that conversations between networks/devices must originate from inside the network, as a device outside of the network originally is unable to target specific devices on the internal network

Configuration
access-list 1 permit any
ip nat pool {PoolName} 100.0.0.1 100.0.0.2 netmask 255.0.0.0
ip nat inside source list 1 pool {PoolName}
interface g0/0/0
ip nat outside - only one `outside` can be defined
interface g0/0/1
ip nat inside




PAT - Port Address Translation
Extends NAT to a more useful range of internal addresses
Allows 100s of internal addresses to translate to one single exterior address
Adds port numbers to keep track of different conversations
Allows external devices to talk to specific internal devices
Allows for multiple conversations to occur at once - parallel processing

Configuration
access-list 1 permit any




Static NAT
Needed to allow for a DMZ-style exterior address for server access
Allows conversations to start from outside the network but only for a specific address
Real internal address stays hidden

Configuration (hide web server behind 192.168.1.5 behind 34.0.0.2 exterior address)
ip nat inside source static 192.168.1.5 34.0.0.2




Default Routing Between Routers
- `ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 <target_ip>`


Enable OSPF
- `router ospf 1`
- `router-id <id>` - ex `router-id 1.1.1.1` for router 1 in a network, allows routers to be distinguished
- `default-information originate`
- `clear ip ospf process`




Loopback Interface Definition
- `interface <port>` - eg `interface Loopback0`
- `ip address <address> <mask>` - look for the IP address attached to the loopback port, eg `ip address 10.0.5.1 255.255.255.0`
- `no shut`
- `exit`